{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "c1",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly states that PostgreSQL transaction IDs have a 32-bit size.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "transaction IDs have limited size (32 bits)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c2",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage ties the 32-bit transaction ID size to wraparound after more than 4 billion transactions, which entails an XID space of about 4 billion values.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "But since transaction IDs have limited size (32 bits) a cluster that runs for a long time (more than 4 billion transactions) would suffer transaction ID wraparound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c3",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage directly states that for every normal XID there are two billion older and two billion newer XIDs.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "This means that for every normal XID, there are two billion XIDs that are “older” and two billion that are “newer”; another way to say it is that the normal XID space is circular with no endpoint."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c4",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly says every table in every database must be vacuumed at least once every two billion transactions.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "To avoid this, it is necessary to vacuum every table in every database at least once every two billion transactions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c5",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages describe catastrophic data loss and say the data is still there but inaccessible, but they do not state that the loss is permanent or unrecoverable.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c6",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages state that anti-wraparound autovacuum is triggered at autovacuum_freeze_max_age, that the default is 200 million transactions, and that the wraparound avoidance window is two billion transactions; 200 million is about 10% of two billion.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "To ensure that this does not happen, autovacuum is invoked on any table that might contain unfrozen rows with XIDs older than the age specified by the configuration parameter autovacuum_freeze_max_age."
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "A value higher than autovacuum_freeze_max_age wouldn't make sense because an anti-wraparound autovacuum would be triggered at that point anyway"
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "(The default, 200 million transactions, translates to about 50MB of pg_xact storage and about 2GB of pg_commit_ts storage.)"
        },
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "To avoid this, it is necessary to vacuum every table in every database at least once every two billion transactions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c7",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "With the default autovacuum_freeze_max_age of 200 million, the stated effective maximum for vacuum_freeze_table_age is 0.95 times that value, i.e. 190 million, not 180 million.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "The effective maximum for vacuum_freeze_table_age is 0.95 * autovacuum_freeze_max_age; a setting higher than that will be capped to the maximum."
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "(The default, 200 million transactions, translates to about 50MB of pg_xact storage and about 2GB of pg_commit_ts storage.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c8",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages say warnings begin at forty million transactions from wraparound and new XIDs are refused once fewer than three million remain, giving a 37 million transaction interval between those thresholds.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "If for some reason autovacuum fails to clear old XIDs from a table, the system will begin to emit warning messages like this when the database's oldest XIDs reach forty million transactions from the wraparound point:"
        },
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "If these warnings are ignored, the system will refuse to assign new XIDs once there are fewer than three million transactions left until wraparound:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c9",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says that after XID assignment stops, read-only transactions can still be started, so it is false that no new transactions of any kind can be started.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 8,
          "quote": "In this condition any transactions already in progress can continue, but only read-only transactions can be started."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "c1",
