    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c3",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly says autovacuum is invoked on tables with sufficiently old unfrozen rows even if autovacuum is disabled.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "To ensure that this does not happen, autovacuum is invoked on any table that might contain unfrozen rows with XIDs older than the age specified by the configuration parameter autovacuum_freeze_max_age. (This will happen even if autovacuum is disabled.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c4",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage gives the formula that with N databases a new worker is launched every autovacuum_naptime/N seconds. With N=4 and autovacuum_naptime=60, this is 15 seconds.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "The launcher will distribute the work across time, attempting to start one worker within each database every autovacuum_naptime seconds. (Therefore, if the installation has N databases, a new worker will be launched every autovacuum_naptime/N seconds.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c5",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states there is no limit on how many autovacuum workers might be in a single database, which contradicts the claim that each database gets at most one worker at a time.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "There is no limit on how many workers might be in a single database, but workers do try to avoid repeating work that has already been done by other workers."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c6",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly says running autovacuum workers do not count towards max_connections.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "Note that the number of running workers does not count towards max_connections or superuser_reserved_connections limits."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c7",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage directly states that autovacuum processes table partitions just like other tables.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "(Autovacuum does process table partitions just like other tables.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c8",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly states that autovacuum does not run ANALYZE on partitioned tables.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "Unfortunately, this means that autovacuum does not run ANALYZE on partitioned tables, and this can cause suboptimal plans for queries that reference partitioned table statistics."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c9",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly states that the autovacuum daemon does not issue ANALYZE commands for foreign tables.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "The autovacuum daemon does not issue ANALYZE commands for foreign tables, since it has no means of determining how often that might be useful."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "c10",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says that while ordinary autovacuum can be interrupted by conflicting lock acquisition, autovacuum running to prevent transaction ID wraparound is not automatically interrupted.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "However, if the autovacuum is running to prevent transaction ID wraparound (i.e., the autovacuum query name in the pg_stat_activity view ends with (to prevent wraparound)), the autovacuum is not automatically interrupted."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

